the mummy is a hugely underrated  adventure movie but that doesn't mean  it's perfect what happened to imitep's  final plague whose idea was that curse  and what's with those egyptian side  characters we have questions  from a contemporary vantage point it's  hard to watch the mummy without noticing  that the movie is very much haunted by  the specter of colonialism in fact the  plot literally wouldn't advance if the  english and american characters didn't  reflexively feel that it was their right  to go to egypt and do basically whatever  they wanted evie and jonathan are  reportedly part egyptian via their  mother but they're played by white  english actors using english accents and  clearly read as essentially english like  many characters in the franchise they  see the riches of egyptian civilization  as both a thing of the past and entirely  up for grabs imitab's rise acts partly  as a commentary on all that when you  blunder into a culture and history you  don't fully understand you can unleash  more than you intend to  no  you must not read from the book you can  also see the lingering presence of  empire in the histories of rick and  winston the former first comes across  haminoptra when he's serving in the  french foreign legion and the latter is  a former british officer hanging around  in egypt because he misses the daring  battles of his glory days egypt isn't  quite home in the mummy it's a place  where you're supposed to have colorful  dangerous adventures that take you out  of your ordinary life and for just about  all the white characters egypt mostly  exists as a playground one in which you  can make your name and find a fortune  it sure is nice that the magi dedicate  their lives to making sure imitep  doesn't rise from the dead but all of  this could have been avoided if their  ancient predecessors simply hadn't used  the dreaded hom die curse in the first  place imitep's priests were mummified  alive isn't that punishment enough for  the man himself did someone really need  to include flesh-eating scarabs and  magical incantations to make imitep's  death just a little bit worse than it  would be already when the consequences  could be so dire if you have two means  of slow gruesome and painful execution  to choose from it seems that you should  always choose the one that can't  potentially backfire and give your enemy  immortality invincibility and the chance  to destroy the world why risk it for one  more twist of the knife the mummy  features several well-written and  prominent egyptian characters artist bae  is skilled charismatic mysterious and  even surprisingly funny meanwhile imitep  is a compelling well-motivated villain  with terrific screen presence even dr  terence bay the museum curator and  evie's beleaguered supervisor has some  great scenes but it's hard to avoid the  fact that a lot of the other egyptian  characters are basically jokes and those  jokes often have a nasty racial  component the warden is an undesirable  travel companion who acts as a constant  butt of the movie's humor it's  particularly uncomfortable that jonathan  keeps saying that he smells bad even his  death by flesh eating scarabs which  sounds horrific in theory it's like a  comedic beat  when imitep's return to life and power  seemingly convinces most of cairo to go  along with him their mass mumbled  chanting and lifeless shambling seem  like a visual gang they're essentially  zombies it's great that the movie boasts  some bold interesting characters of  color it's just unfortunate that many of  the supporting characters fall into some  pretty ugly stereotypes  nobody should quibble too much about the  technical details in a film that  includes mummies resurrection spells and  ancient curses still you may find  yourself asking is the map to hemanoptra  really going to remain intact after more  than 3 000 years actually it turns out  that this isn't as unlikely as it may  seem thousands of pieces of ancient  egyptian papyrus have survived into the  modern day thanks to the arid egyptian  weather while the paper might decay  quickly and more humid environments it's  much safer in the desert but if you've  raised your eyebrows at that pristine  map in the mummy your suspicions weren't  completely unfounded as there's a little  bit of hollywood cleanup going on here  papyrus documents from as far back as 4  500 years ago have survived into the  present day but it's fair to say that  they don't tend to be in sparkling  condition  uncharacteristically when imitep takes  poor mr burns's eyes the mummy misses  the chance for a great if cruel gag burn  says early on that without glasses he  couldn't even see well enough to play  cards and when his glasses are broken in  the mad scramble after imitep rises it  becomes clear that he wasn't  exaggerating his view of the world is a  blurry mess of course burns vision is  about to get even worse as immitep tears  out his eyes and tongue to replace his  own kickstarting his journey back to  life the film only shows the aftermath  but it's surprisingly harrowing it also  inevitably raises a big question if  imitep took burns's eyes shouldn't he  have burns terrible swimmy vision a  cursed mummy who's barely able to see  his own crumbling hand in front of his  decaying face is exactly the kind of  comedic beat the mummy could pull off so  it feels like a shame to miss it the  best the audience gets is a hint as when  imitep is getting used to his new eyes  he squints slightly at eevee which feels  like a nod to burns's poor eyesight but  it's not exactly the payoff that the  earlier setup deserved  what makes imitep evil  from a modern day standpoint it's hard  to condemn the fact that he slept with  the pharaoh's mistress imitep and  anuksunamun were genuinely and  passionately in love while the pharaoh  seemed to only treat her as property  even though it's almost always a bad  idea to dabble in resurrection magic  imitap's initial quest to bring anuksunu  moon back to life feels like a grand  romantic tragedy not the beginning of  descent into super villainy when he  comes back from the dead however he  becomes a literal monster imitable seems  to take pleasure in causing fear and  suffering and his ultimate plan is to  take control of the entire world so why  the change answer the hom die the curse  that was inflicted on him didn't just  give him the potential for super-powered  resurrection it faded him to bring about  death and even all the plagues of egypt  in essence the hom died doomed the world  partly by taking away imitep's free will  chalk that up as another win for the  magi  the mummy is a real delight of a movie  but part of what makes it so enjoyable  is that it never really dwells on any of  the carnage that takes place along the  way that serves the movie's tone well  but after a certain number of re-watches  it's hard not to notice how blind the  protagonists can be about all that death  and destruction  americans  when the warden dies a truly horrific  death they're not shaken so much as  confused within minutes jonathan is  cracking a joke at his expense within an  hour or two rick and evie are having a  flirtatious interlude by the fire the  sam apathy is clear when the american  expedition team starts dying off too at  this point the heroes have interacted  with these people a fair amount both on  and off screen and you might expect them  to feel something about their deaths or  you might think they would feel at least  some guilt at having inadvertently  kick-started imitep's devastating reign  of terror in both cases not so much  how long does someone have to be dead  before it's okay to rob their grave this  may be an awkward question to bring up  at a dinner party but it's also  something to think about while watching  the mummy after all the protagonists  kick off the action by infiltrating a  notoriously forbidden gravesite and  excavating a corpse some of them are  hoping for treasure while others are  just hoping for knowledge but all of  them are trespassing on a sacred  cultural site and disturbing a grave is  that really the proper heroic thing to  be doing honestly the outcome of events  in the mummy suggests that it's probably  not yes the lead characters eventually  escape healthy whole and in possession  of plenty of treasure and sequel  potential but look at what they had to  go through to achieve that happy ending  and look at how many of their fellow  grave robbers got lost along the way  the hom die guaranteed imitep the power  to summon the plagues of egypt and even  before his death he was skilled in the  art of resurrection and those  life-sucking abilities also seem closely  tied to the original curse but where did  the sandstorms come from i love the  whole sand wall trick it was beautifully  bastard when winston is flying rick  ardith bay and jonathan and to try to  rescue evie imitep does some impressive  glowering and conjures up a massive  storm to try to knock the plane out of  the air it's sort of a callback to  something that happens when rick first  came across hemanuptra he witnesses  random bursts of sand flying into the  air and eventually it all seems to form  a giant despairing face it's a great  visual but if you stop to think about it  you might find yourself asking why is  immitep a master of the sand and why can  he control it when he's still  technically dead he can also turn into  sand pouring through a keyhole and even  whisking himself away in a sudden flurry  of wind again it's a cool effect but  where does it actually come from  the prophecy predicts that a resurrected  hemitap will bring the return of the ten  plagues of egypt most of them hit  quickly all the water turns to blood  flies and frogs swarm boils strike most  of the population and an eclipse plots  out the sun most of this takes place on  screen and it's fair to assume that more  is happening elsewhere but this plague  progression still leaves the viewer with  some unanswered questions for starters  why doesn't the main cast get affected  by them evie and jonathan are both half  egyptian and in the mummy returns it's  revealed that rick is actually descended  from the magi all of them have  apparently lived there for years  shouldn't they have at least a few boils  on them but the biggest issue is with  the tenth plague the death of the  firstborn sons the movie never mentions  this one even though it could have  provided some amped up third act stakes  what if they had to race to stop imitep  partly to keep this plague from  devastating egypt and potentially  killing jonathan the source of the  movie's comedic relief instead it just  never comes up making for something of  an odd plot hole  the 1932 boris karloff version of the  mummy was an out and out horror movie  and some of its chills remain in the  1999 update this mummy leans more  towards old-fashioned adventure than  horror but it doesn't hesitate to  incorporate more than a few skin  crawling scenes and some of them are  very dark indeed for one thing if you  have a fear of being buried alive the  prologue is a genuinely torturous  experience seeing imitep and his priests  mummified and sealed into their coffins  is bad enough and the cruel cherry on  top is the later revelation that imitep  stayed conscious long enough to scratch  a message into the inside of his coffin  then there are the scarabs themselves  giant bugs that immediately burrow under  your skin  nobody loves that but the most wrenching  scene might be the sad fate of mr burns  imitep's first victim as if it's not bad  enough that the poor guy loses his eyes  and tongue he then has to unknowingly  entertain his attacker with bandaged  eyes and awkward speech he's clumsy  traumatized and overwhelmingly grateful  for some company and kindness when benny  reveals that prince imitab is actually  there to finish the job you started  burns's terror is devastating and makes 